Bizarre thesis requirements exist everywhere.  At the University of
Utah, we have a uuthesis.sty that has been used for scores of theses,
but we still get problems with the thesis editors on every new thesis.
Among the peculiar rules:

        (a) double spacing (okay for editorial markup, but terrible
            for final copy)
        (b) tables must be preceded and followed by at least 6 lines
            of text, but must also appear after their point of
            reference.   What if there is space to fit the table
            following its reference, but not to get 6 lines more of
            text after it?
        (c) Inverted pyramid shape for chapter title.  God help the
            student with a title "An Analysis of
            Antidisestablishmentarianism".

The list goes on and on, but you get the idea.


